#!/usr/bin/execlineb -P
# `s1000000 n100 T dir`, not `T s1000000 n100 dir`: a timestamp directive
# applies to the output that follows it, so putting T first stamps stdout and
# leaves the file bare. The file is what report reads, and a blocked name has
# no other source of time: CoreDNS's log plugin has no timestamp
# replacement of its own.
#
# s/n: rotate current at 1MB (s6-log's own default is ~100KB) and keep 100
# rotated segments (100MB) rather than the default 10, so a run with a lot
# of DNS traffic doesn't lose the earliest blocked names before report reads them.
# report reads every segment, oldest first, so this is a safety margin, not
# the only thing standing between a busy run and a truncated report.
#
# Each start first appends a line to starts. CoreDNS restarting leaves this
# service running, so a second line means the s6-log before it died with the
# lines it had read from the pipe but not written, and the report marks itself
# incomplete for it (aggregate.ts).
if { install -d -m 0755 /var/run/coredns-log }
if { redirfd -a 1 /var/run/coredns-log/starts echo }
s6-log s1000000 n100 T /var/log/coredns
